WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.046
Welcome to Module 7 of the
Non-Person Identity learning path:

2
00:00:04.046 --> 00:00:07.162
convert to managed
identities and federation.

3
00:00:07.162 --> 00:00:09.720
This module is the first half of Gate 5,

4
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.350
Conversion, with Chapter 5
of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.625
It's where service accounts and
secrets actually get replaced.

6
00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:19.344
You'll plan conversions
in the program's order:

7
00:00:19.344 --> 00:00:23.636
a managed identity for an Azure-hosted
workload, a federated credential

8
00:00:23.636 --> 00:00:28.000
where the host has an issuer, and a
certificate only where neither applies.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:32.658
You'll check each federated credential
against the claims the workload will present,

10
00:00:32.658 --> 00:00:35.938
and test it against a fake token
endpoint before it's created.

11
00:00:35.938 --> 00:00:38.776
Then you'll read each
conversion's parallel run,

12
00:00:38.776 --> 00:00:41.613
and see when the legacy
account may be disabled.

13
00:00:41.613 --> 00:00:47.480
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live tenant.

14
00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:52.555
Why does this need care?

15
00:00:52.555 --> 00:00:57.367
Microsoft Learn's workload identity
federation documentation explains it.

16
00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:01.912
A federated credential tells Microsoft
Entra I D to trust tokens from

17
00:01:01.912 --> 00:01:06.143
an outside issuer, such as GitHub
Actions or a Kubernetes cluster.

18
00:01:06.143 --> 00:01:12.077
Three values must match the workload's
token exactly, case for case: the issuer,

19
00:01:12.077 --> 00:01:17.270
the subject, and the audience, which
for Entra I D is exactly one value,

20
00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:20.793
A P I colon slash slash
Azure A D Token Exchange.

21
00:01:20.793 --> 00:01:22.531
And here's the quiet failure.

22
00:01:22.531 --> 00:01:26.918
Learn says a credential with the wrong
subject is created without any error.

23
00:01:26.918 --> 00:01:30.559
It fails only when the workload
tries to exchange its token,

24
00:01:30.559 --> 00:01:32.492
which may be the day you cut over.

25
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:37.173
So in this module, each federated credential
is checked against the claims the

26
00:01:37.173 --> 00:01:40.990
workload will present, and tested
against a fake token endpoint,

27
00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:42.790
before it's ever created.

28
00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.378
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

29
00:01:49.378 --> 00:01:51.765
which takes about sixty
minutes with the lab.

30
00:01:51.765 --> 00:01:56.290
You'll state the order of targets, and
the finding a target out of order raises.

31
00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.255
You'll name the three values a
federated credential must match,

32
00:02:00.255 --> 00:02:02.390
and the audience Entra I D expects.

33
00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.062
You'll explain why every
federated credential

34
00:02:05.062 --> 00:02:07.665
gets a token exchange
test before it's created.

35
00:02:07.665 --> 00:02:11.768
And you'll read a conversion's cutover
gate and its parallel run status,

36
00:02:11.768 --> 00:02:14.527
and say when the legacy
account may be disabled.

37
00:02:14.527 --> 00:02:16.538
Before you start, finish Module 3,

38
00:02:16.538 --> 00:02:21.377
because the conversions follow the target
and the wave it decides, and Module 4,

39
00:02:21.377 --> 00:02:26.290
because a certificate target is issued
and rotated under its credential rules.

40
00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:31.960
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1 or
later with Pester 5, and no tenant.

41
00:02:35.710 --> 00:02:39.272
Unit 1 is the guide's Step
5, which has five parts.

42
00:02:39.272 --> 00:02:43.710
They're the program's conversion steps,
design guidance, not Microsoft's.

43
00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:47.690
Build the target: a managed
identity with its role assignments,

44
00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.439
a federated credential tested
by an actual token exchange,

45
00:02:51.439 --> 00:02:56.108
a certificate credential from the
vault, a group managed service account,

46
00:02:56.108 --> 00:03:00.623
or, on a Windows Server 2025 domain,
a delegated managed service account.

47
00:03:00.623 --> 00:03:03.772
Give it two human owners
and a rotation schedule.

48
00:03:03.772 --> 00:03:09.205
Run old and new in parallel for thirty
days: the application uses the new identity,

49
00:03:09.205 --> 00:03:12.935
and the legacy account stays
enabled, and you watch both.

50
00:03:12.935 --> 00:03:15.760
Disable the legacy
account; don't delete it.

51
00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.202
For an app registration losing a secret,

52
00:03:18.202 --> 00:03:23.160
remove the secret only after the new
credential has carried production traffic.

53
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:28.022
And record the result: the target, the
date and the disabled object's I D.

54
00:03:28.022 --> 00:03:32.809
The program's dangers table adds one
more: federation trusts too much.

55
00:03:32.809 --> 00:03:37.269
A broad subject lets every workload
on that issuer in, so the program's

56
00:03:37.269 --> 00:03:42.250
guidance is exact subjects, and approval
of each credential as a change.

57
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.513
Unit 2 starts with what
the code depends on.

58
00:03:48.513 --> 00:03:50.813
The order of targets is the program's:

59
00:03:50.813 --> 00:03:55.260
an Azure-hosted workload gets a managed
identity, a host with an issuer,

60
00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.251
such as a pipeline, gets
a federated credential,

61
00:03:58.251 --> 00:04:01.625
and a certificate is used
only where neither applies.

62
00:04:01.625 --> 00:04:06.138
A target out of that order
raises N P W I F oh one.

63
00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:08.978
Then the rules on Microsoft
Learn's page of important

64
00:04:08.978 --> 00:04:12.851
considerations and restrictions
for federated identity credentials.

65
00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:16.575
The match is exact and
case-sensitive, with no wildcards.

66
00:04:16.575 --> 00:04:21.292
An app registration or a user-assigned
managed identity holds at most twenty

67
00:04:21.292 --> 00:04:25.862
federated credentials, and a system-assigned
identity can't hold one at all.

68
00:04:25.862 --> 00:04:30.826
And, as you've heard, a credential with
the wrong subject is created without error.

69
00:04:30.826 --> 00:04:33.960
Everything in the checks
follows from those facts.

70
00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.510
The checks are nine rules,
N P W I F oh one to oh nine.

71
00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:45.685
Oh one is the order, the
program's design guidance.

72
00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:48.776
Oh two, oh three and oh
four are the three values,

73
00:04:48.776 --> 00:04:51.542
from Microsoft Learn's federation pages:

74
00:04:51.542 --> 00:04:56.993
a subject that isn't exactly what the
workload's issuer will present, case for case;

75
00:04:56.993 --> 00:05:01.630
an issuer that isn't exactly the
workload's, a trailing slash included;

76
00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:05.535
and an audience that isn't
exactly the one Entra I D expects.

77
00:05:05.535 --> 00:05:10.227
Oh five and oh six are Microsoft Learn's
limits: a bad name, a wildcard, a

78
00:05:10.227 --> 00:05:13.997
system-assigned parent, more
than twenty credentials,

79
00:05:13.997 --> 00:05:16.510
or the same issuer and subject twice.

80
00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.656
Oh seven is a warning for
a credential someone added

81
00:05:19.656 --> 00:05:22.948
outside the reviewed file;
it's reported, never deleted.

82
00:05:22.948 --> 00:05:25.685
Oh eight is a failed token exchange test.

83
00:05:25.685 --> 00:05:28.767
And oh nine is a parallel
run that isn't clean,

84
00:05:28.767 --> 00:05:32.800
such as an old secret still
signing in after cutover.

85
00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.387
Two pieces make the check real.

86
00:05:38.387 --> 00:05:43.025
First, the code works out the claims the
workload's issuer will put in its token.

87
00:05:43.025 --> 00:05:47.278
For GitHub, Microsoft Learn's
trust page gives the formats:

88
00:05:47.278 --> 00:05:51.000
the repository and an
environment, a branch or a tag.

89
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:53.538
For Kubernetes, it's the service account.

90
00:05:53.538 --> 00:05:56.896
Anything it can't be sure
of, such as a pull request,

91
00:05:56.896 --> 00:06:00.338
has to be captured from a
real test run, not derived.

92
00:06:00.338 --> 00:06:02.399
Second, the exchange test.

93
00:06:02.399 --> 00:06:07.835
It builds the token request from Microsoft
Learn's OAuth 2.0 client credentials flow

94
00:06:07.835 --> 00:06:10.786
page and sends it only to
a fake token endpoint,

95
00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:15.212
which matches exactly and
case-sensitively, the way Learn describes.

96
00:06:15.212 --> 00:06:19.100
It passes only if the named
credential is the one that matched.

97
00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:20.963
Be clear about what that proves.

98
00:06:20.963 --> 00:06:24.012
It shows a credential will
match the claims you recorded.

99
00:06:24.012 --> 00:06:26.995
Whether the real issuer
presents those claims,

100
00:06:26.995 --> 00:06:30.250
and what the real service
answers, is Lab 2's job.

101
00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.812
Unit 3 is the plan and the write path.

102
00:06:36.812 --> 00:06:40.437
Each conversion gets actions,
and only three ever write.

103
00:06:40.437 --> 00:06:43.389
Create identity, for a
user-assigned identity

104
00:06:43.389 --> 00:06:45.875
the file asks for that doesn't exist yet.

105
00:06:45.875 --> 00:06:48.825
Create credential, for
a federated credential

106
00:06:48.825 --> 00:06:51.700
that passes every check
and its exchange test.

107
00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:55.462
And disable legacy, after
a clean thirty-day parallel

108
00:06:55.462 --> 00:06:59.138
run and the owner's approval,
with Disable A D Account.

109
00:06:59.138 --> 00:07:02.359
Everything else is a status,
from awaiting cutover,

110
00:07:02.359 --> 00:07:06.576
to parallel running, to awaiting
the owner's approval, to converted.

111
00:07:06.576 --> 00:07:10.425
The thresholds for a clean run
are the program's design guidance.

112
00:07:10.425 --> 00:07:12.213
The write path is separate.

113
00:07:12.213 --> 00:07:16.059
It runs only the plan whose hash
was approved, one write at a time,

114
00:07:16.059 --> 00:07:20.940
the identity before its credential,
because Microsoft Learn's trust page warns

115
00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.451
that parallel creates under one identity
fail, and it never sends a delete.

116
00:07:25.451 --> 00:07:28.069
And it runs as a person
with Cloud Application

117
00:07:28.069 --> 00:07:31.320
Administrator and Managed
Identity Contributor.

118
